# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DO NOT try to block dev files here. Measured 2026-08-11: Cloudflare Pages
# serves an existing static asset in preference to any _redirects rule, so a
# "/README.md /404.html 404" line does nothing. Proven with a probe rule on a
# path with no file behind it — the probe returned 301 (so the file was live)
# while /README.md kept returning 200 with real content.
#
# Pages publishes everything committed, which currently exposes /_dev/e2e/*,
# /README.md, /package.json, /package-lock.json, /.github/workflows/e2e.yml,
# /partials/* and /index-maintenance. No credentials are in any of them — every
# secret resolves from process.env or GitHub Actions — but the E2E suite maps
# the CRM API surface and names the cleanup endpoint's X-E2E-Secret header.
#
# They cannot be .gitignored either: the e2e workflow runs `node
# _dev/e2e/run.js` from the repo. The fix is a Cloudflare Pages BUILD COMMAND
# that strips them from the deployed output — see README/handover notes.
# robots.txt covers indexing in the meantime, but is not an access control.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Old WordPress URLs → new site (301 permanent redirects)
# Detected via Google Search Console 404 report 2026-04-28

# Direct content matches
/tango-classes/online-tango-classes/  /en/tango-classes/online/  301
/fr/en-ligne/  /fr/cours-de-tango/en-ligne/  301
/en/tango-classes/tango-classes-in-brussels/tango-classes-for-beginners/  /en/tango-classes/beginners/  301

# Old /about pages → homepage (no about page in new site)
/about/  /en/  301
/en/about/  /en/  301

# WordPress default pages
/en/sample-page/  /en/  301
/en/portfolio/  /en/  301
/en/hello-world/  /en/blog/  301

# Old WordPress URLs without /en/ prefix
/contact/  /en/contact/  301
/tango-classes/  /en/tango-classes/  301
/tango-classes/woluwe/  /en/tango-classes/woluwe/  301

# Old blog URLs without the /en/ prefix. Until 404.html existed these returned
# HTTP 200 with the root language-redirect page, so they looked live to search
# engines; now they would 404. Every old slug still matches an EN post, so send
# them to their real home instead of dropping the inbound links.
/blog/  /en/blog/  301
/blog/*  /en/blog/:splat  301

# WordPress wp-content assets — 404 is correct, no redirect needed
# /wp-content/* paths are old media/theme assets that no longer exist

# Note: Query-string redirects (/?p=1, /?page_id=2, /?p=1053)
# are not supported by Cloudflare Pages _redirects file.
# These will naturally stop being crawled over time.
